
> style-studio@1.0.0 test
> tsx --test tests/*.test.ts

✔ same account: a second photo is refused with a retryable 429 and takes no global slot; another account uploads while the first stalls; everything is released (12970.5168ms)
✔ the rule is shared in both directions: a stalled analyze holds the account's token against a photo, and is released at its deadline (3002.0177ms)
✔ queued and refused paths do not leak tokens or slots: wait timeout, full queue, then retries succeed (2066.4342ms)
✔ failing paths release the token at once: bad image, oversize body, client abort, header-valid but undecodable image, then success (1119.9372ms)
✔ direct admission: same-id refusal is a RequestError 429 with a retry hint, a throwing job releases, ids are independent (84.6482ms)
✔ the set of holding accounts never exceeds the existing image active + queue limits, and drains completely (57.9242ms)
✔ a client abort while queued removes the waiter at once: no wait, no busy count, token released (56.2022ms)
✔ the guard ends before the database write in both routes (static source order) (3.2986ms)
✔ /api/health/uploads exposes only fixed-shape integers and no identifiers (331.8773ms)
2026-10-18T12:03:48.371Z WARN [Better Auth]: Invalid password
✔ verified accounts isolate wardrobes and photos, handle conflicts, recover and delete themselves (12147.8528ms)
✔ image-work semaphore bounds concurrency, queue and wait, and always releases (2850.403ms)
✔ upload inspection rejects by header metadata before any decode (245.8673ms)
✔ limits are configurable and size is judged on decoded bytes (91.7534ms)
✔ a burst of oversize and over-quota uploads is refused cheaply and boundedly (187.4232ms)
✔ time spent waiting for the gate is deducted from the SQLite budget, and the failed begin releases the gate (680.1852ms)
✔ a gate wait that is shorter than the budget still leaves the rest for the SQLite lock, which is then acquired (580.994ms)
✔ a gate wait that uses the whole budget fails as a gate timeout: no BEGIN is attempted and nothing is left held (151.1921ms)
✔ STATIC: begin() derives the SQLite timeout from one deadline and the shared retry accepts an explicit remaining budget (1.3748ms)
✔ consistent backup excludes temporary photos and restore preserves account deletions (16481.8026ms)
✔ only 'this file system cannot fsync' is tolerated; I/O failures are not (2.1922ms)
✔ a real I/O error while flushing the backup file fails the backup: nothing published, no partial, no sensitive bytes (1511.87ms)
✔ a file system that cannot fsync is tolerated and reported as a platform limit, not as a failure (1246.8986ms)
✔ a real I/O error flushing the directory after publication keeps the verified backup but reports it as not durable, and old backups are not pruned (2797.3069ms)
✔ a platform that cannot flush a directory is reported as a limit and does not block retention (3554.6595ms)
✔ the real flushes: a platform that can flush a directory does so; Windows reports the documented limit (3179.4887ms)
✔ NATIVE hard-link publication: when the partial's name cannot be removed after the final name exists, the backup is reported as published with the leftover named, not as failed; the next backup sweeps it (4154.4988ms)
﹣ REAL fallback: where this environment refuses hard links, publication uses rename, the partial name is gone, nothing is left over, and the unlink step never runs (0.4834ms) # hard links work here, so the native branch is what runs (see the native test)
✔ REACTION (injected link refusal, any platform): a refused hard link falls back to rename and the backup is complete with no leftover (2804.4293ms)
✔ REACTION (injected; a copy stands in for the link, so this is NOT hard-link or file-system proof): link succeeds, removing the partial name fails -> published with the leftover named, durable, next backup sweeps it; a taken name moves on to -1 (3529.0676ms)
✔ rate-limit rows (client addresses) never reach a completed backup; the table is kept, empty (2330.8088ms)
﹣ POSIX: a pre-existing wide backups directory is narrowed to 0700 (that directory only) and the backup files are owner-only (0.4264ms) # POSIX mode bits do not exist on Windows
✔ the overlap predicate counts only writes whose whole interval is strictly inside the copy (12.1923ms)
﹣ a backup of a database of 300 MB or more completes within the bound while another process commits writes inside the copy, and passes an integrity check (0.5077ms) # resource-intensive: set STYLE_LARGE_BACKUP_TEST=1 to run
✔ a completed backup is sanitised, verified and published under a final name only (7090.9715ms)
✔ a failure at any stage leaves no final file, no partial and no marker bytes, and keeps the earlier backup and the live data (4674.6444ms)
✔ pruning happens only after a backup completed (2198.9254ms)
✔ a cleanup that cannot remove the partial says so instead of claiming it is gone (1507.1203ms)
✔ a killed owner leaves only a partial that restore refuses; its lock dies with it, the next backup takes over at once and removes the dead partial (10319.4521ms)
✖ two contenders waiting on a live owner cannot take its lock; when it is killed they proceed one at a time and both succeed (12746.9698ms)
Skipped coordinator link guard: this account may not create links.
✔ the lock module: a second holder is refused while the first lives (same process too), granted after release, and the coordinator file is guarded (437.9802ms)
✔ a LIVE owner whose main thread is blocked for a long time keeps its lock and its partial and completes (the first 009 failure) (13503.2354ms)
✔ publication never replaces an existing backup, even when the name is taken at the last moment (1336.1266ms)
✔ two backups started in the same millisecond are serialised and never replace an earlier backup (11081.8822ms)
✔ physical path guards: a linked backups directory is refused and a linked partial name is unlinked without touching its target (1154.8134ms)
